{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788201722,"nonce":"X2hGjMASvyj3kFo3f8YDKFUJk80=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCONFIRMED — every checkable claim in both amendments resolves. Three additions,\none of which removes a perceived blocker.\n\nWHAT I CONFIRMED BY READING\n\n1. The undraft hole is real and the vanilla evidence is exactly as cited.\n   `Data/Core/Defs/Tutor/Instructions.xml` has `UndraftAll` as its own step\n   (second from last, after `DownRaider`). The `Drafting` concept lives in\n   `Data/Core/Defs/Tutor/Concepts_NotedOpportunistic.xml` and its `helpText`\n   reads, verbatim: \"A drafted colonist will do only what you tell them, and can\n   only take combat actions. Those with weapons will use them when possible.\\n\\n\n   Don't leave colonists Drafted forever - they'll starve and become unhappy.\"\n   Both halves of the amendment's quote are accurate. CONFIRMED.\n\n   Precision note on \"A drafted colonist does not eat, sleep or work\": that is\n   the GAME'S DOCUMENTATION, quoted above, not something I traced through the\n   think tree. The code-level mechanism is the drafted branch of\n   `Data/Core/Defs/ThinkTreeDefs/Humanlike.xml` (the `DraftedOrder` job tag).\n   I did not walk it. Treat the behaviour as well-attested rather than\n   source-confirmed, and note that the FIX does not depend on the distinction.\n\n2. `undraft` already ships as a verb (`Source/AutoRimmer/PawnOrderVerbs.cs`,\n   `[Verb(\"undraft\")] ... =\u003e DraftImpl(ctx, false)`). So the hole is purely\n   procedural — the loop document has to say when to call it. Nothing is blocked.\n   CONFIRMED.\n\n3. The `activeOp` warning is exactly right, and I can point at the ordering.\n   `Source/AutoRimmer/AgentGameComponent.cs`: `PublishSnapshot()` is called\n   before `DrainCommands()` in the same `GameComponentUpdate`; inside the drain,\n   `activeOp = cmd.Op;` is set and `activeOp = null;` is cleared around the\n   dispatch; and the snapshot line is\n   `activeOp = TimeDriver.Active ? \"advance:\" + TimeDriver.ActiveId : activeOp`.\n   So by the time any snapshot is written, a short command's `activeOp` has\n   already been cleared — only `null` or `advance:\u003cid\u003e` can ever be observed.\n   CONFIRMED, mechanism and conclusion.\n\n4. The `until:` matcher set. `Source/AutoRimmer/TimeDriver.cs`:\n   `private enum Until { None, Ticks, Letter, Threat, Alert, Event }`, and the\n   parse ends `else throw new VerbArgsException(\"until needs one of: letter,\n   threat, alert, event\");`. No `condition`. CONFIRMED — write the loop's guards\n   against these five.\n\n5. 1.7 shipped and the payload is as described. `TimeDriver` has\n   `Halt(\"dialog\", ForcePausePayload(stack), ...)`, `data[\"halted_on\"] = haltEvent`,\n   and a `ForcePausePayload(WindowStack)` that walks windows for\n   `if (w == null || !w.forcePause) continue;`. CONFIRMED.\n\n6. The `pawn` section count. `Source/AutoRimmer/PawnSerializer` declares exactly\n   thirteen: identity, state, needs, mood, health, skills, apparel, equipment,\n   inventory, schedule, work, area, relations. The \"all 13 sections\" phrasing is\n   accurate. The BYTE figures (8.7KB / 1.9KB / ~120 bytes a line) are\n   measurements and I did not re-measure them — see the inference note below.\n\nADDITION 1 — THE LOOP NEEDS A SECOND EXIT CONDITION, NOT ONE, AND THE SECOND ONE\nIS THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY WEDGES A RUN. The amendment fixes draft-without-undraft.\nThere is a structurally identical hole one layer down that no amendment names:\nafter `advance` halts with `reason:\"dialog\"`, EVERY subsequent advance halts at\n0 ticks until the window is cleared. 4.3's session-5 amendment says this; 4.2's\ntext does not, and 4.2 is the document that describes what the loop DOES each\nturn. Comment #2's last line calls `reason:\"dialog\"` \"a first-class turn input\"\nand then defers the handling to 3.5. That is right about ownership and wrong\nabout the loop's obligation: the loop must have a rule for what to do when 3.5\nis not yet available (halt the session and escalate — which is exactly what the\n\"Escalation to Dorian\" bullet is for) rather than looping on a zero-tick advance.\nRecommend an explicit termination rule: *N consecutive advances returning 0 ticks\nends the session and escalates.* Without it the transcript fills with identical\nturns and the run looks alive.\n\nADDITION 2 — \"checklist items always produce either 'ok' or an action; silent\nskips forbidden\" is the right invariant and is UNCHECKABLE from the artifacts as\nscoped. Nothing in the deliverables list records the checklist's own output in a\nform a reviewer can diff against the checklist's item list. Recommend the\nmorning-checklist artifact be one line per item ID with a verdict, so \"silent\nskip\" becomes a missing line rather than a judgement about prose. That also makes\n4.4's budget/retirement ledger (d32eadd) computable from run artifacts instead of\nfrom memory — the two specs want the same file.\n\nADDITION 3 — THE COST NOTE IN COMMENT #2 IS GOOD ADVICE AND HAS A TRAP UNDER IT.\n\"Opinions are opt-in (`opinions:true`) and perturb bounded state — leave them off\nin routine loops\" is correct, and there is a second read in the routine path with\nthe same property that the note does not mention: the digest's per-colonist\n`room` field calls `pawn.GetRoom()` and then `r.Role`, and `Verse/Room.Role` is\n`get { if (statsAndRoleDirty) { UpdateRoomStatsAndRole(); } return role; }` — a\nfull room-stat and role recomputation on read. It is idempotent and RNG-free, so\nit is legal under the observer rule, but it is not free and it is in the\nper-turn staple rather than the drill-down. Worth a sentence in the cadence\nsection: the digest is cheap in BYTES and not uniformly cheap in WORK.\n\nACCEPTANCE — TESTABLE, WITH ONE CLAUSE THAT NEEDS A DEFINITION\n\n\"A scripted one-day session on a fixture colony: checklist artifacts produced, at\nleast one checklist-driven action taken, transcript complete, summary written.\nReviewed against the skill text for compliance.\"\n\n- \"transcript complete\" has no completeness criterion. Make it mechanical: the\n  transcript contains every `rwa` call the session made, and the count matches\n  the journal's own dev/verb record for the window.\n- \"at least one checklist-driven action taken\" is testable and good — it is the\n  clause that makes this more than a document review.\n- \"Reviewed against the skill text for compliance\" is a human gate. That is\n  fine and should be labelled as one, the way 4.3 labels Dorian's sign-off.\n- With the undraft amendment in, the acceptance should exercise it: a scripted\n  one-day session that includes a threat and ends with zero drafted colonists is\n  a one-line addition and it closes the amendment's own loop.\n\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n\n- The three byte measurements in comment #2 (8.7KB / 1.9KB / ~120 bytes a line).\n  I confirmed the section COUNT and the shape; I did not re-run the measurement.\n- That a drafted colonist will not auto-eat. The concept text says so; I did not\n  read the think tree. See point 1.\n- Everything in Additions 1-3 is a recommendation, not a finding about the game.","files":null}]}